{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2e8bff0980bd42bfbe86d3e6f8e2733f","ordinal":721,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.221","text":"\n\n⟨...⟩ sacrifice a human being to Cronus, which most people consider impious. In Alexandria they sacrifice a cat to Horus, and to Thetis a beetle—something no one among us would do. They offer a horse to Poseidon with good omens; but to Apollo, especially Apollo of Didyma, the animal is hateful. To sacrifice goats to Artemis is pious, but not to Asclepius."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-70d03155b8204afca8d4cdcea5a3f38f","ordinal":722,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.222","text":"\n\nI could cite a great many more examples like these, but I leave them aside, aiming at brevity. Yet if any sacrifice were holy or unholy by nature, it would be considered so by everyone alike. Similar things can also be found in people’s religious observances about diet."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9df4bceb0ed04225809f153d55842a3d","ordinal":723,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.223","text":"\n\nA Jew or an Egyptian priest would sooner die than eat pork; for a Libyan, tasting mutton seems among the most unlawful of things; for some Syrians, tasting dove, and for others, sacrificial victims. In some cults it is lawful to eat fish, in others impious. And among the Egyptians who are reputed to be wise, some think it unholy to eat an animal’s head, others the shoulder blade, others the foot, others an onion."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-917ccb4d5735480397d983ff6cead028","ordinal":724,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.224","text":"\n\nNo one consecrated to Zeus Casius at Pelusium would take an onion, just as a priest of Aphrodite in Libya would not taste garlic. In some temples they abstain from mint, in others from garden mint, in others from celery. Some say they would sooner eat their fathers’ heads than beans. But among others these things are indifferent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-28919d484b04402ba718ee5e302a1402","ordinal":725,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.225","text":"\n\nWe think it unholy to taste dog meat, but some Thracians are reported to eat dogs. Perhaps this was customary among the Greeks too; that is why Diocles, drawing on the Asclepiads’ teachings, prescribes that puppy meat be given to certain patients. And some, as I said, eat human flesh without concern, which among us is considered unholy.\n〔1〕 Diocles of Carystus was a fourth-century BC physician; the Asclepiads were the medical guild that traced itself back to Asclepius."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-92027b7b741e435a9734078057a9bd38","ordinal":726,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.226","text":"\n\nYet if matters of worship and of what is unlawful were so by nature, they would be considered the same by everyone. Similar things can be said about reverence toward the dead. Some wrap the dead completely and cover them with earth, thinking it impious to show them to the sun; but the Egyptians take out the entrails, embalm them, and keep them above ground with them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e378add9100348a1a6d6028000e2e144","ordinal":727,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.227","text":"\n\nThe Fish-eaters of Ethiopia throw them into the lakes to be eaten by the fish; the Hyrcanians put them out as food for dogs, and some Indians for vultures. The Troglodytes, they say, take the dead person to a hill, tie his head to his feet, and pelt him with stones, laughing; then, when they have buried him under the stones they have thrown, they go away."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-30ab06d29162459c89c49fda3ac9e82d","ordinal":728,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.228","text":"\n\nSome foreign peoples sacrifice and eat those who are over sixty, but bury in the earth those who die young. Some burn their dead; of these, some gather up the bones and give them burial, while others carelessly leave them lying scattered. The Persians, they say, impale their dead and embalm them with natron, and then wrap them in bandages. And we see how much grief others endure over the dead."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-10cdb795b061430f82565677007389e5","ordinal":729,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.229","text":"\n\nDeath itself, too, some think terrible and to be avoided, others not. Euripides, for one, says: “Who knows whether living is being dead, / and being dead is counted living down below?” And Epicurus says: “Death is nothing to us; for what has been dissolved has no sensation, and what has no sensation is nothing to us.” They also say that if we are composed of soul and body, and death is the dissolution of soul and body, then when we exist death does not exist (for we are not being dissolved), and when death exists we do not exist; for since the combination of soul and body no longer exists, neither do we."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0258bda708d3496ca4fccb3cc0504e1f","ordinal":730,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.230","text":"\n\nHeraclitus says that both living and dying are present both in our living and in our being dead. For when we live, our souls are dead and buried in us; and when we die, our souls come back to life and live. Some even suppose that dying is better for us than living. Euripides, for one, says: “We ought to gather together\nand mourn the newborn for all the ills he is entering,\nbut the one who has died and ceased from troubles\nto send forth from the house with joy and words of blessing.” And these lines too were spoken from the same view:"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-973ad700b9d64be68ff4d9c53b3dee94","ordinal":731,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.231","text":"\n\n“Best of all for mortals is never to be born, / nor to see the rays of the piercing sun; but once born, to pass as quickly as may be through the gates of Hades, and lie with much earth heaped over one.” And we know the story of Cleobis and Biton, which Herodotus tells in his account of the priestess of Argos.\n〔1〕 Herodotus (1.31): after the brothers drew their mother, a priestess of Hera, to the temple by cart, the goddess granted them the best gift for mortals—death in their sleep."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3a41cd9a1849476b88625cacd49eebd6","ordinal":732,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.232","text":"\n\nSome Thracians, too, are reported to sit around a newborn child and lament. So death would not be considered one of the things that are terrible by nature, just as living is not one of the things that are fine by nature. Nor, then, is any of the things mentioned above by nature of this kind or that; all are matters of convention and relative."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b87f97cf849c472c87992acf84da9cfb","ordinal":733,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.233","text":"\n\nThe same method of argument can be carried over to each of the other cases, which we have not set out now, to keep the account brief. And even if there are some matters about which we cannot point to variation straight away, we must say that it is possible that there is disagreement about these too among some peoples unknown to us."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-bd756268900e4e868613badb0865b39e","ordinal":734,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.234","text":"\n\nIf, say, we did not know the Egyptian custom of marrying sisters, we would be wrong to affirm that it is agreed by everyone that one should not marry one’s sisters. In the same way, about those matters in which no variations come to our notice, it is not proper to affirm that there is no disagreement about them, since it is possible, as I said, that among some of the peoples unknown to us there is disagreement about them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8b76b88992f746fc96cfc91561f7393c","ordinal":735,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.235","text":"\n\nThe skeptic, then, seeing so much variation in things, suspends judgment about whether anything is by nature good or bad, or in general to be done, and in this he keeps clear of the dogmatists’ rashness. He follows the observance of everyday life without holding beliefs, and for this reason he remains unaffected in matters of belief, while in matters forced upon him he has moderate feeling."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2ed0675c341c453d9e627e2f33fcac12","ordinal":736,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.236","text":"\n\nFor as a human being with senses he is affected; but since he does not add the belief that what he is undergoing is bad by nature, his feeling is moderate. For adding a belief of that kind is worse than the actual undergoing: sometimes people who are being operated on, or undergoing something of the sort, bear it, while the bystanders faint because of their belief that what is happening is bad."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e9bfb56e6336469ab6c582425022e168","ordinal":737,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.237","text":"\n\nBut the person who has posited that something is by nature good or bad, or in general to be done or not to be done, is troubled in all sorts of ways. When the things he thinks bad by nature are present to him, he thinks he is being hounded by avengers; and when he gets hold of what seem to him good things, he falls into no ordinary troubles, both from conceit and from fear of losing them, [and] being anxious that he may again find himself among the things he considers bad by nature."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-995a94577b214bfaa9760498fb494085","ordinal":738,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.238","text":"\n\nAs for those who say that goods cannot be lost, we shall bring them to a standstill with the impasse that arises from the disagreement. Hence we conclude that if what produces something bad is bad and to be avoided, and the conviction that these things are good by nature and those bad produces troubles, then positing and being convinced that anything is bad or good in its nature is bad and to be avoided. That is enough, for now, about good, bad and indifferent things."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c81b057027f54be3876b91dcc200ff03","ordinal":739,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.239","text":"\n\nIt is clear from what has been said that there could not be any art of living either. For if there is such an art, it is concerned with the study of good, bad and indifferent things; so, since these do not exist, the art of living does not exist either. Besides, since the dogmatists do not all agree in recognizing a single art of living, but different ones posit different arts, they fall under the disagreement and the argument from disagreement that I put forward in what we said about the good."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-544257dd7e41432080b3194bb59c52c8","ordinal":740,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.240","text":"\n\nBut even if, for the sake of hypothesis, they all said that the art of living is one—for instance, the celebrated practical wisdom that the Stoics dream about, which seems more impressive than the others—absurdities will follow just the same. For since practical wisdom is an excellence, and only the wise man had excellence, the Stoics, not being wise, will not"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-217174ce97aa4c9db36e36cc987658c9","ordinal":741,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.241","text":"\n\nhave the art of living. And in general, since according to them an art cannot exist, there will not be an art of living either, as far as what they say goes. For instance, they say that an art is a system of apprehensions, and that an apprehension is assent to an apprehensive impression. But the apprehensive impression cannot be found. For not every impression is apprehensive, nor can it be recognized which of the impressions is the apprehensive one, since we cannot simply judge by any impression whatever which is apprehensive and which is not. And since we need an apprehensive impression in order to recognize which is the apprehensive impression, we are thrown into an infinite regress, demanding another apprehensive impression to recognize the one taken to be apprehensive."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ba2574cc4cc240b1a7badf2d5475b747","ordinal":742,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.242","text":"\n\nThat is why the Stoics too go astray in setting out the concept of the apprehensive impression. They say that an apprehensive impression is one that comes from what is real, and that what is real is what is capable of producing an apprehensive impression; and so they fall into the mode of reciprocity. If, then, for there to be an art of living there must first be an art, and for an art to exist apprehension must already exist, and for apprehension to exist assent to an apprehensive impression must have been apprehended, but the apprehensive impression cannot be found, then the art of living cannot be found."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-060851003ba244b3a9e5bc1e6bdc4ff7","ordinal":743,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.243","text":"\n\nThis too is said. Every art seems to be apprehended from the works it specifically produces; but there is no work specific to the art of living. For whatever one says is its work turns out to be common to ordinary people as well—honoring one’s parents, for instance, returning deposits, and all the rest. So there is no art of living. Nor shall we recognize that something is a work of practical wisdom from the fact that it appears to be done or produced by the practically wise man from a wise disposition (as some say)."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3de633c42a084b17949bdff047ac34f2","ordinal":744,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.244","text":"\n\nFor the wise disposition itself is inapprehensible, since it appears neither from itself, simply and directly, nor from its works; for these are common to ordinary people too. And to say that we apprehend the person who has the art of living by the consistency of his actions is the talk of people who overrate human nature and are praying rather than telling the truth: “For such is the mind of men upon the earth / as the day the father of men and gods brings on them.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-92ef07b5f50241549bd5b9878a3962d9","ordinal":745,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.245","text":"\n\nIt remains to say that the art of living is apprehended from the works they record in their books. Since these are many and similar to one another, I shall set out a few by way of example. For instance, the founder of their school, Zeno, in his Discourses, says this, among other similar things, about the upbringing of boys: “Have intercourse between the thighs no more and no less with a beloved than with one who is not a beloved, nor with females than with males; for it makes no difference whether with beloveds or not, or with females or males, but the same things are fitting and suitable.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-df17c6ed2e80481a9b72710a1585eedc","ordinal":746,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.246","text":"\n\nOn piety toward parents, the same man says, with reference to the story of Jocasta and Oedipus, that there was nothing terrible in his rubbing his mother. “If, when she was ill, he had helped her by rubbing some other part of her body with his hands, there would be nothing shameful in it. Then if he gave her pleasure by rubbing other parts, putting an end to her distress, and begot noble children by his mother, was that shameful?” Chrysippus agrees with this. In his Republic, at any rate, he says: “It seems to me right to arrange these matters too in the way that is customary even now, and not wrongly, among many peoples: so that a mother has children by her son, a father by his daughter, and a brother by his sister born of the same mother.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e42e83830a9c41e4a9397d501f72d0c7","ordinal":747,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.247","text":"\n\nAnd in the same treatises he ⟨repeatedly⟩ introduces ⟨even⟩ cannibalism to us. At any rate he says: “And if a part useful for food is cut off from the living, we should neither bury it nor throw it away in some other manner, but consume it, so that from our parts another part may come into being.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8ac9c51fc20e4ee897a3b087564b7b10","ordinal":748,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.248","text":"\n\nIn his work On What Is Appropriate, on the burial of parents, he says explicitly: “When our parents have passed away, we should use the simplest burials, since the body, like nails or teeth or hair, is nothing to us, and we do not need to pay any such attention or care. So if the flesh is useful, they will use it for food, just as with one’s own parts—for instance, if a foot is cut off, it would be fitting to use it and the like. But if it is useless, they will either bury it and leave it, or burn it and let the ashes go, or throw it far away and pay it no more attention than a nail or hair.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-46a148b91d074ff3a9e319cbcb638543","ordinal":749,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.249","text":"\n\nThe philosophers say a great many things of this sort—things they would not dare to put into practice unless they lived as citizens among the Cyclopes or the Laestrygonians. And if they never act on these at all, while what they do is common to ordinary people too, there is no work specific to those suspected of having the art of living. So if arts must in every case be apprehended from their specific works, and no specific work is seen for the so-called art of living, it is not apprehended. That is why no one can affirm about it that it is real.\n〔1〕 The Cyclopes and Laestrygonians are the man-eating giants of Homer’s Odyssey."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-bf20d9dcab064bb398cd7d087f78d45f","ordinal":750,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.250","text":"\n\nMoreover, if the art of living arises in human beings, it arises in them either by nature or through learning and teaching. If by nature, the art of living would arise in them either insofar as they are human beings or insofar as they are not human beings. Insofar as they are not human beings, not at all—for it is not the case that they are not human beings. If insofar as they are human beings, then practical wisdom would belong to all human beings, so that all would be practically wise, excellent and wise."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ed694960049a45c5a9da313ab243a13a","ordinal":751,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.251","text":"\n\nBut they say that most people are bad. So the art of living would not belong to them insofar as they are human beings either; and so not by nature. Besides, since they hold that an art is a system of apprehensions exercised together, they suggest rather that the other arts, and the one under discussion too, are acquired through some experience and learning."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1481180970a745038580b144dbfd62b5","ordinal":752,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.252","text":"\n\nBut it is not acquired by teaching and learning either. For these to exist, three things must first be agreed: the thing taught, the teacher and the learner, and the method of learning. But none of these exists; so neither does teaching."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a84ab156d6ed43d882c97dd7cb567ac2","ordinal":753,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.253","text":"\n\nFor instance, what is taught is either true or false. If it is false, it would not be taught; for they say the false does not exist, and there could be no teaching of what does not exist. Nor if it were said to be true; for we pointed out in our discussion of the criterion that the true does not exist. So if neither the false nor the true is taught, and apart from these nothing is teachable (for surely no one will say that, while these cannot be taught, people teach only the puzzling things), then nothing is taught."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-cd0f5c7fcb1248938096c0e7ca381d5c","ordinal":754,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.254","text":"\n\nAgain, the thing taught is either apparent or non-evident. If it is apparent, it will not need teaching, for what appears appears to everyone alike. If it is non-evident, it will not be teachable, since non-evident things are inapprehensible because of the undecidable disagreement about them, as we have often pointed out; for how could anyone teach or learn what he does not apprehend? And if neither what appears nor what is non-evident is taught, nothing is taught."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-37484cddf1ff44bf9bf1aa87a918278f","ordinal":755,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.255","text":"\n\nFurther, what is taught is either a body or incorporeal; and each of these, being either apparent or non-evident, cannot be taught, by the argument we gave a little earlier."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4eec44f9559a4c538e432901c3f4c0d7","ordinal":756,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.256","text":"\n\nSo nothing is taught. Moreover, either what is is taught or what is not. Now what is not is not taught. For if what is not is taught, then, since teachings are thought to be of truths, what is not will be true. And being true, it will also be real; for they say that the true is what is real and is opposed to something. But it is absurd to say that what is not is real. So what is not is not taught."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-745c2b996eab4224b96f78b7e33a9e02","ordinal":757,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.257","text":"\n\nBut neither is what is. For if what is is taught, it is taught either insofar as it is or in virtue of something else. If it is teachable insofar as it ⟨is⟩, it will be one of the things that are, and for that reason not teachable; for teaching must proceed from things that are agreed and need no teaching. So what is is not teachable insofar as it is. Nor, indeed, in virtue of something else."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5c4be028a6474b3683587d49ac54a6b5","ordinal":758,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.258","text":"\n\nFor what is has nothing else accidental to it that is not. So if what is is not taught insofar as it is, it will not be taught in virtue of anything else either; for whatever is accidental to it is. Besides, whether the being they will say is taught is apparent or non-evident, it falls under the puzzles already stated and will be unteachable. And if neither what is nor what is not is taught, there is nothing that is taught."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9d72f21b81564708b4f072da74dd4cbc","ordinal":759,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.259","text":"\n\nThe teacher and the learner are overturned along with this; but they are no less puzzling taken on their own. For either the expert teaches the expert, or the non-expert the non-expert, or the non-expert the expert, or the expert the non-expert. Now the expert does not teach the expert; for neither of them, insofar as he is an expert, needs learning. Nor does the non-expert teach the non-expert, any more than the blind can lead the blind. Nor the non-expert the expert:"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d66e81a2e79c4c428bae5a0e864bd11a","ordinal":760,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.260","text":"\n\nthat would be ridiculous. It remains to say that the expert teaches the non-expert; but this too is impossible. For it is said to be altogether impossible for an expert to exist, since no one is seen to be an expert spontaneously, from birth, nor does anyone become an expert from being a non-expert. For either one theorem and a single apprehension can make the non-expert an expert, or not at all."}]}